  "worked_solution": "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t+1}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2t+1}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2t+1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7}} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7} & =2t+1 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7})}^{2} & ={(2t+1)}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+7 & =4{t}^{2}+4t+1 \\\\ 0 & =3{t}^{2}+3t-6 \\\\ 0 & ={t}^{2}+t-2 \\\\ 0 & =(t-1)(t+2) \\\\ t & =1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $t=1$ since $t\\ge 0.$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=1,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+1+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+7} \\\\  & =3\\text{\u00a0m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $v=3\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
